    content: "# What Historians Can Know About Deep Time\n\n*Grade 6 World History · Unit 2 of 9 · Early Humans and Farming · Lesson 1*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** distinguish evidence-based claims from guesses about early human history.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students use models, vocabulary, and evidence. Prefer labeled diagrams and CER sentences over one-word answers.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nLook at a library primary-source folder. Write one question a scientist could ask that today’s idea (“What Historians Can Know About Deep Time”) might help answer.\n\nShare with a partner: What prior idea from earlier in Unit 2 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday’s skill — **What Historians Can Know About Deep Time** — sits inside **Early Humans and Farming**. Grade 6 history is not a pile of trivia dates; it is a set of habits for explaining change over time with evidence. A correct vocabulary word with no map, timeline, or source is weaker than a clear explanation a classmate can follow.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when reading news, visiting historic sites, talking about citizenship, or understanding how Texas connects to the wider world. At Prosper Prep we connect careful historical thinking to scholarship habits: define terms, use maps and timelines, cite evidence, and revise when better sources appear.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **domestication** — use this word with a definition in your own sentence, not as decoration.\n- **foraging** — pair it with an example from a library primary-source folder.\n- **Evidence** beats slogans. Prefer “The source shows… which supports…” over “because history says so.”\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a library primary-source folder. We want to understand something connected to “What Historians Can Know About Deep Time.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we trying to explain or decide?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** Map, timeline, cause/effect chart, T-chart, or primary-source quote + paraphrase — pick what matches the question.\n**Step 3 — Apply the science idea.** Connect evidence to the definition of domestication. If dates or quantities help (for example 6 and 9), label them clearly.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Ask: Does this contradict a known pattern? Did we confuse a common mistake like: using modern country borders as if they always existed?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I defined the key terms first. I drew a quick model of a library primary-source folder and labeled domestication and foraging. My evidence was that when conditions changed, the pattern matched the lesson prediction. I checked for the common mix-up (using modern country borders as if they always…) and revised my labels so a classmate could follow the reasoning.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nKeep the mathematical/scientific structure of “What Historians Can Know About Deep Time” but switch the context to a scholarship essay about civic responsibility.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Write two evidence sentences.\n4. Write a concluding claim that a skeptic could test.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature vocabulary.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** using modern country borders as if they always existed.\n- **Also watch for:** copying a diagram without labels; treating a model as reality; confusing correlation with a full causal explanation.\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire explanation blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nMap skills and geographic vocabulary set up every later unit’s place-based explanations.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — In the context of a library primary-source folder, restate today’s goal (“What Historians Can Know About Deep Time”) in your own words, then list two pieces of evidence you would collect.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Goal restates Distinguish evidence-based claims from guesses about early h… Evidence should be specific or clearly describable with units/labels.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Use the terms **domestication** and **foraging** correctly in two sentences that could appear in a history notebook.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Sentences show accurate definitions and connect to the lesson phenomenon; no circular “it is what it is.”\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student wrote only “because history” with no source or map. What two follow-up questions should you ask before accepting the answer?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear maps/timelines + evidence sentences.\n\n1. Problem 1: Apply “What Historians Can Know About Deep Time” to a short scenario about a Sunday afternoon conversation about heritage. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 11, -1, 43.)\n2. Problem 2: Apply “What Historians Can Know About Deep Time” to a short scenario about a courthouse square monument. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 6, 1, 26.)\n3. Problem 3: Apply “What Historians Can Know About Deep Time” to a short scenario about a cattle-drive history display. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 6, 4, 45.)\n4. Problem 4: Apply “What Historians Can Know About Deep Time” to a short scenario about a timeline poster in the classroom. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 11, 1, 20.)\n5. Problem 5: Apply “What Historians Can Know About Deep Time” to a short scenario about a library primary-source folder. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 11, 4, 39.)\n6. Problem 6: Apply “What Historians Can Know About Deep Time” to a short scenario about a museum map wall. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 15, 6, 22.)\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your map/timeline and evidence, not only a final word.*\n\n1. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Distinguish evidence-based claims from guesses abo…\n2. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Distinguish evidence-based claims from guesses abo…\n3. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Distinguish evidence-based claims from guesses abo…\n4. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Distinguish evidence-based claims from guesses abo…\n5. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Distinguish evidence-based claims from guesses abo…\n6. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Distinguish evidence-based claims from guesses abo…\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “What Historians Can Know About Deep Time” let you explain that you could not explain as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint related to: using modern country borders as if…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) about “What Historians Can Know About Deep Time” using a library primary-source folder. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
