    content: "# Technology and Tools Over Time\n\n*Grade 6 World History · Unit 2 of 9 · Early Humans and Farming · Lesson 7*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** interpret simple tool changes as evidence of problem-solving, not “smarter vs less smart.”\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students use models, vocabulary, and evidence. Prefer labeled diagrams and CER sentences over one-word answers.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nTrue or false (guess OK): “accepting a social media claim about history without asking for a source” — write why you agree or disagree in one sentence.\n\nShare with a partner: What prior idea from earlier in Unit 2 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday’s skill — **Technology and Tools Over Time** — sits inside **Early Humans and Farming**. Grade 6 history is not a pile of trivia dates; it is a set of habits for explaining change over time with evidence. A correct vocabulary word with no map, timeline, or source is weaker than a clear explanation a classmate can follow.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when reading news, visiting historic sites, talking about citizenship, or understanding how Texas connects to the wider world. At Prosper Prep we connect careful historical thinking to scholarship habits: define terms, use maps and timelines, cite evidence, and revise when better sources appear.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **settlement** — use this word with a definition in your own sentence, not as decoration.\n- **agriculture** — pair it with an example from an East Texas family road trip past historic markers.\n- **Evidence** beats slogans. Prefer “The source shows… which supports…” over “because history says so.”\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine an East Texas family road trip past historic markers. We want to understand something connected to “Technology and Tools Over Time.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we trying to explain or decide?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** Map, timeline, cause/effect chart, T-chart, or primary-source quote + paraphrase — pick what matches the question.\n**Step 3 — Apply the science idea.** Connect evidence to the definition of settlement. If dates or quantities help (for example 6 and -2), label them clearly.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Ask: Does this contradict a known pattern? Did we confuse a common mistake like: accepting a social media claim about history without asking for a source?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I defined the key terms first. I drew a quick model of an East Texas family road trip past historic markers and labeled settlement and agriculture. My evidence was that when conditions changed, the pattern matched the lesson prediction. I checked for the common mix-up (accepting a social media claim about history without…) and revised my labels so a classmate could follow the reasoning.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nKeep the mathematical/scientific structure of “Technology and Tools Over Time” but switch the context to a scholarship essay about civic responsibility.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Write two evidence sentences.\n4. Write a concluding claim that a skeptic could test.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature vocabulary.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** accepting a social media claim about history without asking for a source.\n- **Also watch for:** copying a diagram without labels; treating a model as reality; confusing correlation with a full causal explanation.\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire explanation blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nMap skills and geographic vocabulary set up every later unit’s place-based explanations.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — In the context of an East Texas family road trip past historic markers, restate today’s goal (“Technology and Tools Over Time”) in your own words, then list two pieces of evidence you would collect.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Goal restates Interpret simple tool changes as evidence of problem-solving… Evidence should be specific or clearly describable with units/labels.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Use the terms **settlement** and **agriculture** correctly in two sentences that could appear in a history notebook.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Sentences show accurate definitions and connect to the lesson phenomenon; no circular “it is what it is.”\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student wrote only “because history” with no source or map. What two follow-up questions should you ask before accepting the answer?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear maps/timelines + evidence sentences.\n\n1. Problem 1: Apply “Technology and Tools Over Time” to a short scenario about an East Texas family road trip past historic markers. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 11, 6, 49.)\n2. Problem 2: Apply “Technology and Tools Over Time” to a short scenario about a timeline poster in the classroom. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 11, 10, 28.)\n3. Problem 3: Apply “Technology and Tools Over Time” to a short scenario about a library primary-source folder. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 11, 13, 47.)\n4. Problem 4: Apply “Technology and Tools Over Time” to a short scenario about a courthouse square monument. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 10, 14, 34.)\n5. Problem 5: Apply “Technology and Tools Over Time” to a short scenario about a cattle-drive history display. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 10, 9, 13.)\n6. Problem 6: Apply “Technology and Tools Over Time” to a short scenario about a scholarship essay about civic responsibility. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 10, 12, 32.)\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your map/timeline and evidence, not only a final word.*\n\n1. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Interpret simple tool changes as evidence of probl…\n2. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Interpret simple tool changes as evidence of probl…\n3. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Interpret simple tool changes as evidence of probl…\n4. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Interpret simple tool changes as evidence of probl…\n5. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Interpret simple tool changes as evidence of probl…\n6. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Interpret simple tool changes as evidence of probl…\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Technology and Tools Over Time” let you explain that you could not explain as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint related to: accepting a social media claim about…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) about “Technology and Tools Over Time” using an East Texas family road trip past historic markers. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
