    content: "# Mesopotamia: Cities and Writing\n\n*Grade 6 World History · Unit 3 of 9 · River Civilizations · Lesson 2*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** describe city life, irrigation, and early writing in Mesopotamia.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students use models, vocabulary, and evidence. Prefer labeled diagrams and CER sentences over one-word answers.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nTrue or false (guess OK): “reducing the Columbian Exchange to “new foods only” and ignoring disease and forced labor” — write why you agree or disagree in one sentence.\n\nShare with a partner: What prior idea from earlier in Unit 3 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday’s skill — **Mesopotamia: Cities and Writing** — sits inside **River Civilizations**. Grade 6 history is not a pile of trivia dates; it is a set of habits for explaining change over time with evidence. A correct vocabulary word with no map, timeline, or source is weaker than a clear explanation a classmate can follow.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when reading news, visiting historic sites, talking about citizenship, or understanding how Texas connects to the wider world. At Prosper Prep we connect careful historical thinking to scholarship habits: define terms, use maps and timelines, cite evidence, and revise when better sources appear.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **scribe** — use this word with a definition in your own sentence, not as decoration.\n- **monument** — pair it with an example from a scholarship essay about civic responsibility.\n- **Evidence** beats slogans. Prefer “The source shows… which supports…” over “because history says so.”\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a scholarship essay about civic responsibility. We want to understand something connected to “Mesopotamia: Cities and Writing.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we trying to explain or decide?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** Map, timeline, cause/effect chart, T-chart, or primary-source quote + paraphrase — pick what matches the question.\n**Step 3 — Apply the science idea.** Connect evidence to the definition of scribe. If dates or quantities help (for example 4 and 5), label them clearly.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Ask: Does this contradict a known pattern? Did we confuse a common mistake like: reducing the Columbian Exchange to “new foods only” and ignoring disease and forced labor?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I defined the key terms first. I drew a quick model of a scholarship essay about civic responsibility and labeled scribe and monument. My evidence was that when conditions changed, the pattern matched the lesson prediction. I checked for the common mix-up (reducing the Columbian Exchange to “new foods only”…) and revised my labels so a classmate could follow the reasoning.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nKeep the mathematical/scientific structure of “Mesopotamia: Cities and Writing” but switch the context to a world atlas open on the kitchen table.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Write two evidence sentences.\n4. Write a concluding claim that a skeptic could test.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature vocabulary.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** reducing the Columbian Exchange to “new foods only” and ignoring disease and forced labor.\n- **Also watch for:** copying a diagram without labels; treating a model as reality; confusing correlation with a full causal explanation.\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire explanation blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nEarly humans and river civilizations show how environment and innovation shaped complex societies.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — In the context of a scholarship essay about civic responsibility, restate today’s goal (“Mesopotamia: Cities and Writing”) in your own words, then list two pieces of evidence you would collect.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Goal restates Describe city life, irrigation, and early writing in Mesopot… Evidence should be specific or clearly describable with units/labels.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Use the terms **scribe** and **monument** correctly in two sentences that could appear in a history notebook.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Sentences show accurate definitions and connect to the lesson phenomenon; no circular “it is what it is.”\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student wrote only “because history” with no source or map. What two follow-up questions should you ask before accepting the answer?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear maps/timelines + evidence sentences.\n\n1. Problem 1: Apply “Mesopotamia: Cities and Writing” to a short scenario about a courthouse square monument. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 11, -3, 42.)\n2. Problem 2: Apply “Mesopotamia: Cities and Writing” to a short scenario about a Sunday afternoon conversation about heritage. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 9, 5, 19.)\n3. Problem 3: Apply “Mesopotamia: Cities and Writing” to a short scenario about a scholarship essay about civic responsibility. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 11, 3, 40.)\n4. Problem 4: Apply “Mesopotamia: Cities and Writing” to a short scenario about an East Texas family road trip past historic markers. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 9, 3, 17.)\n5. Problem 5: Apply “Mesopotamia: Cities and Writing” to a short scenario about a museum map wall. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 11, 2, 38.)\n6. Problem 6: Apply “Mesopotamia: Cities and Writing” to a short scenario about a library primary-source folder. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 9, 2, 15.)\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your map/timeline and evidence, not only a final word.*\n\n1. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Describe city life, irrigation, and early writing …\n2. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Describe city life, irrigation, and early writing …\n3. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Describe city life, irrigation, and early writing …\n4. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Describe city life, irrigation, and early writing …\n5. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Describe city life, irrigation, and early writing …\n6. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Describe city life, irrigation, and early writing …\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Mesopotamia: Cities and Writing” let you explain that you could not explain as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint related to: reducing the Columbian Exchange to “new…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) about “Mesopotamia: Cities and Writing” using a scholarship essay about civic responsibility. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
