    content: "# Classical Mediterranean Snapshot\n\n*Grade 6 World History · Unit 4 of 9 · Classical Empires and Belief Systems · Lesson 2*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** outline key features of classical Mediterranean political life at Grade 6 depth.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students use models, vocabulary, and evidence. Prefer labeled diagrams and CER sentences over one-word answers.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout looking anything up, sketch what you think “province” means. Then star one part you are least sure about.\n\nShare with a partner: What prior idea from earlier in Unit 4 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday’s skill — **Classical Mediterranean Snapshot** — sits inside **Classical Empires and Belief Systems**. Grade 6 history is not a pile of trivia dates; it is a set of habits for explaining change over time with evidence. A correct vocabulary word with no map, timeline, or source is weaker than a clear explanation a classmate can follow.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when reading news, visiting historic sites, talking about citizenship, or understanding how Texas connects to the wider world. At Prosper Prep we connect careful historical thinking to scholarship habits: define terms, use maps and timelines, cite evidence, and revise when better sources appear.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **province** — use this word with a definition in your own sentence, not as decoration.\n- **citizen** — pair it with an example from a timeline poster in the classroom.\n- **Evidence** beats slogans. Prefer “The source shows… which supports…” over “because history says so.”\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a timeline poster in the classroom. We want to understand something connected to “Classical Mediterranean Snapshot.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we trying to explain or decide?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** Map, timeline, cause/effect chart, T-chart, or primary-source quote + paraphrase — pick what matches the question.\n**Step 3 — Apply the science idea.** Connect evidence to the definition of province. If dates or quantities help (for example 9 and 0), label them clearly.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Ask: Does this contradict a known pattern? Did we confuse a common mistake like: treating a single map as the whole truth about a region?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I defined the key terms first. I drew a quick model of a timeline poster in the classroom and labeled province and citizen. My evidence was that when conditions changed, the pattern matched the lesson prediction. I checked for the common mix-up (treating a single map as the whole truth…) and revised my labels so a classmate could follow the reasoning.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nKeep the mathematical/scientific structure of “Classical Mediterranean Snapshot” but switch the context to a library primary-source folder.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Write two evidence sentences.\n4. Write a concluding claim that a skeptic could test.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature vocabulary.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** treating a single map as the whole truth about a region.\n- **Also watch for:** copying a diagram without labels; treating a model as reality; confusing correlation with a full causal explanation.\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire explanation blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nEarly humans and river civilizations show how environment and innovation shaped complex societies.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — In the context of a timeline poster in the classroom, restate today’s goal (“Classical Mediterranean Snapshot”) in your own words, then list two pieces of evidence you would collect.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Goal restates Outline key features of classical Mediterranean political li… Evidence should be specific or clearly describable with units/labels.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Use the terms **province** and **citizen** correctly in two sentences that could appear in a history notebook.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Sentences show accurate definitions and connect to the lesson phenomenon; no circular “it is what it is.”\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student wrote only “because history” with no source or map. What two follow-up questions should you ask before accepting the answer?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear maps/timelines + evidence sentences.\n\n1. Problem 1: Apply “Classical Mediterranean Snapshot” to a short scenario about a timeline poster in the classroom. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 7, 6, 24.)\n2. Problem 2: Apply “Classical Mediterranean Snapshot” to a short scenario about an East Texas family road trip past historic markers. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 9, 5, 45.)\n3. Problem 3: Apply “Classical Mediterranean Snapshot” to a short scenario about a museum map wall. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 11, 12, 26.)\n4. Problem 4: Apply “Classical Mediterranean Snapshot” to a short scenario about a world atlas open on the kitchen table. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 14, 13, 39.)\n5. Problem 5: Apply “Classical Mediterranean Snapshot” to a short scenario about a Prosper Prep history seminar table. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 7, 12, 20.)\n6. Problem 6: Apply “Classical Mediterranean Snapshot” to a short scenario about a Sunday afternoon conversation about heritage. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 9, 10, 41.)\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your map/timeline and evidence, not only a final word.*\n\n1. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Outline key features of classical Mediterranean po…\n2. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Outline key features of classical Mediterranean po…\n3. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Outline key features of classical Mediterranean po…\n4. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Outline key features of classical Mediterranean po…\n5. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Outline key features of classical Mediterranean po…\n6. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Outline key features of classical Mediterranean po…\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Classical Mediterranean Snapshot” let you explain that you could not explain as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint related to: treating a single map as the…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) about “Classical Mediterranean Snapshot” using a timeline poster in the classroom. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
