    content: "# Medieval Europe Snapshot\n\n*Grade 6 World History · Unit 5 of 9 · Medieval Networks · Lesson 2*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** outline feudal relationships, manors, and church influence at intro level.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students use models, vocabulary, and evidence. Prefer labeled diagrams and CER sentences over one-word answers.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout looking anything up, sketch what you think “crossroads” means. Then star one part you are least sure about.\n\nShare with a partner: What prior idea from earlier in Unit 5 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday’s skill — **Medieval Europe Snapshot** — sits inside **Medieval Networks**. Grade 6 history is not a pile of trivia dates; it is a set of habits for explaining change over time with evidence. A correct vocabulary word with no map, timeline, or source is weaker than a clear explanation a classmate can follow.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when reading news, visiting historic sites, talking about citizenship, or understanding how Texas connects to the wider world. At Prosper Prep we connect careful historical thinking to scholarship habits: define terms, use maps and timelines, cite evidence, and revise when better sources appear.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **crossroads** — use this word with a definition in your own sentence, not as decoration.\n- **scholarship** — pair it with an example from a courthouse square monument.\n- **Evidence** beats slogans. Prefer “The source shows… which supports…” over “because history says so.”\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a courthouse square monument. We want to understand something connected to “Medieval Europe Snapshot.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we trying to explain or decide?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** Map, timeline, cause/effect chart, T-chart, or primary-source quote + paraphrase — pick what matches the question.\n**Step 3 — Apply the science idea.** Connect evidence to the definition of crossroads. If dates or quantities help (for example 10 and 6), label them clearly.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Ask: Does this contradict a known pattern? Did we confuse a common mistake like: leaving Texas out of U.S. and world connections as if it floated alone?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I defined the key terms first. I drew a quick model of a courthouse square monument and labeled crossroads and scholarship. My evidence was that when conditions changed, the pattern matched the lesson prediction. I checked for the common mix-up (leaving Texas out of U.S. and world connections…) and revised my labels so a classmate could follow the reasoning.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nKeep the mathematical/scientific structure of “Medieval Europe Snapshot” but switch the context to a world atlas open on the kitchen table.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Write two evidence sentences.\n4. Write a concluding claim that a skeptic could test.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature vocabulary.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** leaving Texas out of U.S. and world connections as if it floated alone.\n- **Also watch for:** copying a diagram without labels; treating a model as reality; confusing correlation with a full causal explanation.\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire explanation blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nEmpires, belief systems, and medieval networks show how ideas and goods traveled — and how power was organized.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — In the context of a courthouse square monument, restate today’s goal (“Medieval Europe Snapshot”) in your own words, then list two pieces of evidence you would collect.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Goal restates Outline feudal relationships, manors, and church influence a… Evidence should be specific or clearly describable with units/labels.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Use the terms **crossroads** and **scholarship** correctly in two sentences that could appear in a history notebook.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Sentences show accurate definitions and connect to the lesson phenomenon; no circular “it is what it is.”\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student wrote only “because history” with no source or map. What two follow-up questions should you ask before accepting the answer?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear maps/timelines + evidence sentences.\n\n1. Problem 1: Apply “Medieval Europe Snapshot” to a short scenario about a timeline poster in the classroom. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 6, 3, 30.)\n2. Problem 2: Apply “Medieval Europe Snapshot” to a short scenario about a cattle-drive history display. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 4, 3, 47.)\n3. Problem 3: Apply “Medieval Europe Snapshot” to a short scenario about a courthouse square monument. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 6, 2, 28.)\n4. Problem 4: Apply “Medieval Europe Snapshot” to a short scenario about a Sunday afternoon conversation about heritage. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 12, 7, 13.)\n5. Problem 5: Apply “Medieval Europe Snapshot” to a short scenario about a scholarship essay about civic responsibility. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 14, 6, 34.)\n6. Problem 6: Apply “Medieval Europe Snapshot” to a short scenario about a world atlas open on the kitchen table. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 12, 6, 11.)\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your map/timeline and evidence, not only a final word.*\n\n1. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Outline feudal relationships, manors, and church i…\n2. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Outline feudal relationships, manors, and church i…\n3. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Outline feudal relationships, manors, and church i…\n4. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Outline feudal relationships, manors, and church i…\n5. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Outline feudal relationships, manors, and church i…\n6. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Outline feudal relationships, manors, and church i…\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Medieval Europe Snapshot” let you explain that you could not explain as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint related to: leaving Texas out of U.S. and…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) about “Medieval Europe Snapshot” using a courthouse square monument. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
