    content: "# Contact in the Americas\n\n*Grade 6 World History · Unit 6 of 9 · Exploration and the First Global Age · Lesson 3*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** describe early contact between Europeans and Indigenous peoples with care and evidence.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students use models, vocabulary, and evidence. Prefer labeled diagrams and CER sentences over one-word answers.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nLook at a world atlas open on the kitchen table. Write one question a scientist could ask that today’s idea (“Contact in the Americas”) might help answer.\n\nShare with a partner: What prior idea from earlier in Unit 6 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday’s skill — **Contact in the Americas** — sits inside **Exploration and the First Global Age**. Grade 6 history is not a pile of trivia dates; it is a set of habits for explaining change over time with evidence. A correct vocabulary word with no map, timeline, or source is weaker than a clear explanation a classmate can follow.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when reading news, visiting historic sites, talking about citizenship, or understanding how Texas connects to the wider world. At Prosper Prep we connect careful historical thinking to scholarship habits: define terms, use maps and timelines, cite evidence, and revise when better sources appear.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **forced labor** — use this word with a definition in your own sentence, not as decoration.\n- **maritime** — pair it with an example from a world atlas open on the kitchen table.\n- **Evidence** beats slogans. Prefer “The source shows… which supports…” over “because history says so.”\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a world atlas open on the kitchen table. We want to understand something connected to “Contact in the Americas.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we trying to explain or decide?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** Map, timeline, cause/effect chart, T-chart, or primary-source quote + paraphrase — pick what matches the question.\n**Step 3 — Apply the science idea.** Connect evidence to the definition of forced labor. If dates or quantities help (for example 6 and -3), label them clearly.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Ask: Does this contradict a known pattern? Did we confuse a common mistake like: saying ancient people were simply “dumb” because their tools looked different?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I defined the key terms first. I drew a quick model of a world atlas open on the kitchen table and labeled forced labor and maritime. My evidence was that when conditions changed, the pattern matched the lesson prediction. I checked for the common mix-up (saying ancient people were simply “dumb” because their…) and revised my labels so a classmate could follow the reasoning.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nKeep the mathematical/scientific structure of “Contact in the Americas” but switch the context to a scholarship essay about civic responsibility.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Write two evidence sentences.\n4. Write a concluding claim that a skeptic could test.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature vocabulary.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** saying ancient people were simply “dumb” because their tools looked different.\n- **Also watch for:** copying a diagram without labels; treating a model as reality; confusing correlation with a full causal explanation.\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire explanation blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nEmpires, belief systems, and medieval networks show how ideas and goods traveled — and how power was organized.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — In the context of a world atlas open on the kitchen table, restate today’s goal (“Contact in the Americas”) in your own words, then list two pieces of evidence you would collect.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Goal restates Describe early contact between Europeans and Indigenous peop… Evidence should be specific or clearly describable with units/labels.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Use the terms **forced labor** and **maritime** correctly in two sentences that could appear in a history notebook.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Sentences show accurate definitions and connect to the lesson phenomenon; no circular “it is what it is.”\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student wrote only “because history” with no source or map. What two follow-up questions should you ask before accepting the answer?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear maps/timelines + evidence sentences.\n\n1. Problem 1: Apply “Contact in the Americas” to a short scenario about a world atlas open on the kitchen table. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 6, 4, 37.)\n2. Problem 2: Apply “Contact in the Americas” to a short scenario about a museum map wall. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 6, 7, 16.)\n3. Problem 3: Apply “Contact in the Americas” to a short scenario about an East Texas family road trip past historic markers. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 6, 11, 35.)\n4. Problem 4: Apply “Contact in the Americas” to a short scenario about a courthouse square monument. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 6, 14, 14.)\n5. Problem 5: Apply “Contact in the Americas” to a short scenario about a cattle-drive history display. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 15, 9, 33.)\n6. Problem 6: Apply “Contact in the Americas” to a short scenario about a scholarship essay about civic responsibility. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 15, 5, 12.)\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your map/timeline and evidence, not only a final word.*\n\n1. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Describe early contact between Europeans and Indig…\n2. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Describe early contact between Europeans and Indig…\n3. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Describe early contact between Europeans and Indig…\n4. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Describe early contact between Europeans and Indig…\n5. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Describe early contact between Europeans and Indig…\n6. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Describe early contact between Europeans and Indig…\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Contact in the Americas” let you explain that you could not explain as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint related to: saying ancient people were simply “dumb”…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) about “Contact in the Americas” using a world atlas open on the kitchen table. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
