    content: "# Nation, Empire, and Power\n\n*Grade 6 World History · Unit 7 of 9 · Revolutions and Industry · Lesson 7*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** connect industrial power to imperialism in a Grade 6-appropriate survey.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students use models, vocabulary, and evidence. Prefer labeled diagrams and CER sentences over one-word answers.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout looking anything up, sketch what you think “rights” means. Then star one part you are least sure about.\n\nShare with a partner: What prior idea from earlier in Unit 7 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday’s skill — **Nation, Empire, and Power** — sits inside **Revolutions and Industry**. Grade 6 history is not a pile of trivia dates; it is a set of habits for explaining change over time with evidence. A correct vocabulary word with no map, timeline, or source is weaker than a clear explanation a classmate can follow.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when reading news, visiting historic sites, talking about citizenship, or understanding how Texas connects to the wider world. At Prosper Prep we connect careful historical thinking to scholarship habits: define terms, use maps and timelines, cite evidence, and revise when better sources appear.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **rights** — use this word with a definition in your own sentence, not as decoration.\n- **imperialism** — pair it with an example from a courthouse square monument.\n- **Evidence** beats slogans. Prefer “The source shows… which supports…” over “because history says so.”\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a courthouse square monument. We want to understand something connected to “Nation, Empire, and Power.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we trying to explain or decide?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** Map, timeline, cause/effect chart, T-chart, or primary-source quote + paraphrase — pick what matches the question.\n**Step 3 — Apply the science idea.** Connect evidence to the definition of rights. If dates or quantities help (for example 5 and 1), label them clearly.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Ask: Does this contradict a known pattern? Did we confuse a common mistake like: leaving Texas out of U.S. and world connections as if it floated alone?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I defined the key terms first. I drew a quick model of a courthouse square monument and labeled rights and imperialism. My evidence was that when conditions changed, the pattern matched the lesson prediction. I checked for the common mix-up (leaving Texas out of U.S. and world connections…) and revised my labels so a classmate could follow the reasoning.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nKeep the mathematical/scientific structure of “Nation, Empire, and Power” but switch the context to an East Texas family road trip past historic markers.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Write two evidence sentences.\n4. Write a concluding claim that a skeptic could test.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature vocabulary.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** leaving Texas out of U.S. and world connections as if it floated alone.\n- **Also watch for:** copying a diagram without labels; treating a model as reality; confusing correlation with a full causal explanation.\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire explanation blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nEmpires, belief systems, and medieval networks show how ideas and goods traveled — and how power was organized.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — In the context of a courthouse square monument, restate today’s goal (“Nation, Empire, and Power”) in your own words, then list two pieces of evidence you would collect.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Goal restates Connect industrial power to imperialism in a Grade 6-appropr… Evidence should be specific or clearly describable with units/labels.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Use the terms **rights** and **imperialism** correctly in two sentences that could appear in a history notebook.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Sentences show accurate definitions and connect to the lesson phenomenon; no circular “it is what it is.”\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student wrote only “because history” with no source or map. What two follow-up questions should you ask before accepting the answer?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear maps/timelines + evidence sentences.\n\n1. Problem 1: Apply “Nation, Empire, and Power” to a short scenario about a museum map wall. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 6, 9, 28.)\n2. Problem 2: Apply “Nation, Empire, and Power” to a short scenario about a world atlas open on the kitchen table. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 8, 7, 49.)\n3. Problem 3: Apply “Nation, Empire, and Power” to a short scenario about a Prosper Prep history seminar table. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 10, 6, 30.)\n4. Problem 4: Apply “Nation, Empire, and Power” to a short scenario about a cattle-drive history display. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 12, 13, 11.)\n5. Problem 5: Apply “Nation, Empire, and Power” to a short scenario about a courthouse square monument. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 14, 11, 32.)\n6. Problem 6: Apply “Nation, Empire, and Power” to a short scenario about a library primary-source folder. Include a labeled map, timeline, or T-chart and one evidence sentence. (Optional dates/numbers if helpful: 16, 10, 13.)\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your map/timeline and evidence, not only a final word.*\n\n1. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Connect industrial power to imperialism in a Grade…\n2. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Connect industrial power to imperialism in a Grade…\n3. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Connect industrial power to imperialism in a Grade…\n4. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Connect industrial power to imperialism in a Grade…\n5. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Connect industrial power to imperialism in a Grade…\n6. Strong responses define key terms, show a map/timeline/chart, and cite evidence tied to Connect industrial power to imperialism in a Grade…\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Nation, Empire, and Power” let you explain that you could not explain as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint related to: leaving Texas out of U.S. and…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) about “Nation, Empire, and Power” using a courthouse square monument. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
